That is not correct- examining the code which makes this determination
from pray.c:

            int action = rn1(Luck + (on_altar() ? 3 + on_shrine() : 2), 1);

            if (!on_altar()) action = min(action, 3);
            if (u.ualign.record < STRIDENT)
                action = (u.ualign.record > 0 || !rnl(2)) ? 1 : 0;

Observe that if you have decent alignment, reasonable luck, and are not
on an altar, action can still be 3 (since the first line results in
 1<=action<=Luck, and the second line limits it to three).  The code
which uses this effect then does:


            switch(min(action,5)) {
            case 5: pat_on_head = 1;
            case 4: do fix_worst_trouble(trouble);
                    while ((trouble = in_trouble()) != 0);
                    break;

            case 3: fix_worst_trouble(trouble);
            case 2: while ((trouble = in_trouble()) > 0)
                    fix_worst_trouble(trouble);
                    break;

            case 1: if (trouble > 0) fix_worst_trouble(trouble);
            case 0: break; /* your god blows you off, too bad */
            }

Now, 5 is required to get something good, and 4 is required to fix *all*
your troubles. but 3 is sufficient to fix your worst trouble, then
continue fixing troubles until you run out of major ones.  This
means that if being blind is your worst trouble to start with,
your god will fix it with probability (Luck-2)/Luck for Luck>=3.


